# Rust
/target/
*.profraw

# Cargo.lock IS committed, deliberately.
#
# It was ignored, and that made the C2 transitive-dependency snapshot a time
# bomb: the snapshot records exact patch versions, so on any machine that has
# not already resolved, an upstream patch release reddens `cargo test -p
# mlmf-core` and `scripts/check-deps.sh` with a diff nobody made. A reviewer
# hit it in a fresh worktree — `syn v3.0.3 -> v3.0.4` — while this working
# copy stayed green off a stale lock, so "all gates green" was a property of
# one developer's resolve rather than of the branch.
#
# A gate that fires on someone else's release, forever, on every clean
# checkout, is a gate that gets deleted by whoever is holding it when it
# finally becomes inconvenient. Committing the lock makes the resolve
# reproducible, so the snapshot means what it claims: the set changed
# because someone changed it.
#
# Cargo ignores a library's lockfile when it is consumed as a dependency, so
# this constrains only local and CI builds — which is exactly the scope the
# gate operates in.

